Here is the Architecture Decision Record (ADR) capturing this zero-trust integration pattern. It avoids deep code syntax and focuses entirely on the structural topology, cryptographic handshakes, and platform governance mechanics.
Architecture Decision Record (ADR)
Title: Zero-Trust Workload Identity Federation for Ephemeral Coder Workspaces via GitLab Components
Status: Proposed / Approved
Context Date: May 2026
Author: Platform Engineering Team
1. Context & Problem Statement
Our platform engineering team requires a secure mechanism to inject sensitive, scope-restricted GitLab Personal Access Tokens (PATs) into ephemeral Coder developer workspaces. These workspaces run highly autonomous tasks using advanced software generation agents like Claude Code.
The Traditional Anti-Pattern
Typically, a pipeline runner passes sensitive API variables down into an execution environment via standard plain-text transport layers, static secrets, or long-lived keys. If an autonomous AI agent or an underlying script leaks the environmental block or stores tracking files incorrectly inside the container workspace, the credentials become exposed, broadening the organization's blast radius.
Architectural Objectives
Zero Static Credentials: No pre-shared or long-lived infrastructure access tokens can be stored on the GitLab runner host or inside the persistent Coder database profiles.
Identity Attestation: Every network interface calling the backend infrastructure must cryptographically prove exactly who it is and how it was executed.
End-to-End Encryption: Credentials must remain opaque while traveling across network planes, decrypting only inside the dedicated target workspace memory space.
2. Proposed Design Strategy
We are implementing an asymmetric, workload-federated identity broker model. This architecture relies on an automated Bootstrap Service, a platform-managed GitLab CI/CD Component, and the native cryptographic key generation functions embedded directly into Coder.
